window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["wj"]=[{"id":"front-bumper-wj","title":"Steel Front Bumper for the WJ Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wj","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumpers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":380,"high":950},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","sockets","torque wrench","pry bar"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","bumper removal/install"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["armor","bumper","front bumper","winch plate","recovery","unibody"],"parts":[{"name":"Armor Works WJ front bumper with winch plate","vendor":"Armor Works","price_estimate_usd":780},{"name":"Rough Country WJ front bumper","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Iron Cross Patriot front bumper (WJ)","vendor":"Iron Cross","price_estimate_usd":620},{"name":"Frame horn reinforcement brackets (WJ)","vendor":"GenRight or Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"3/4\" rated D-ring shackle (pair)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":28}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Grand Cherokee Front Bumper Guide \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"},{"title":"WJ Armor Works Bumper Install \u2014 Pirate4x4","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com"}],"alternatives":["rear-bumper-wj","rock-sliders-wj","winch-wj"],"summary":"The WJ's plastic factory bumper cover provides zero protection and no recovery attachment. A steel front bumper with integrated winch plate and D-ring tabs is the single highest-value armor upgrade on a WJ build.","description":"The factory WJ front end is a plastic cover over a thin stamped metal bumper bar \u2014 it crumples on moderate rock contact and provides no recovery attachment. On a vehicle that weighs over 4,200 lbs on the trail, a reliable winch mount and front recovery point are not optional equipment on anything more challenging than a forest road.\n\nThe WJ's unibody construction means the bumper mounts to the front frame horns \u2014 the forward structural extensions of the unibody. These horns are the same elements that rust badly on northern WJs (see rust-wj), so inspect them before installing any heavy front bumper or winch. A steel bumper on rusted frame horns is not safe recovery hardware.\n\n**Armor Works WJ front bumper ($780):** The benchmark WJ bumper. Armor Works designed it specifically for WJ mounting geometry \u2014 the frame horn brackets fit correctly without shimming or drilling new holes. It includes a full-width winch plate rated to 12,000 lbs and integrated D-ring tabs. The Armor Works bumper is heavier (around 140 lbs) and requires two people to position for install, but the fitment is superior to budget alternatives.\n\n**Iron Cross Patriot ($620):** A mid-tier option with a winch-capable center section and stubby wings. Lighter than the Armor Works at around 105 lbs. Fitment is good on the WJ; the narrower design improves approach angle.\n\n**Rough Country WJ bumper ($380):** The entry-level steel bumper. Functional and significantly cheaper, but the steel gauge is thinner and the finish (typically a spray-on powder coat) does not hold up as well in the field. Acceptable for mild trail use; not the right choice for a rock crawling build.\n\nAny bumper with a winch plate puts significant dynamic load on the front frame horns during a recovery pull. The factory frame horns are adequate for straight-back pulls with a snatch strap, but a winching load \u2014 sustained cable tension at 8,000\u201312,000 lbs \u2014 is different in character. Frame horn reinforcement brackets from GenRight or Rock Hard 4x4 weld gussets to the inner face of each horn and significantly improve resistance to bending or tearing under winch load. Install these before relying on the winch on a serious trail.\n\nRemove the factory plastic bumper cover, plastic impact absorber foam, and the factory bumper bar (4 bolts). Clean and inspect the frame horn mounting surfaces. Position the steel bumper with a helper \u2014 the Armor Works bumper in particular is heavy enough to be dangerous to manage alone. Torque the frame horn mounting bolts to spec (typically 65\u201375 ft-lbs). Connect the winch wiring before the bumper is fully seated if installing a winch simultaneously \u2014 the wiring route is easier with the bumper slightly out from the vehicle.\n\nAfter install, check approach angle by turning the front wheels to full lock and inspecting clearance between the tire and bumper wing. On a 2\" lift with 33s, most WJ steel bumpers clear adequately; at 4\" lift and 35s, trimming of the front inner fender or cutting the bumper wing corners may be required."},{"id":"rear-bumper-wj","title":"Rear Bumper with Swing-Out Tire Carrier \u2014 WJ Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wj","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumpers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":450,"high":1100},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","sockets","torque wrench","drill","level"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","bumper install"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["armor","bumper","rear bumper","swing-out","tire carrier","recovery","receiver hitch"],"parts":[{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 WJ rear bumper with swing-out","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":850},{"name":"Smittybilt SRC rear bumper (WJ)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"2\" receiver D-ring adapter","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Extended brake line (WJ rear, for lift)","vendor":"Rough Country or Skyjacker","price_estimate_usd":28}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Rear Bumper and Swing-Out Install \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"}],"alternatives":["front-bumper-wj","recovery-points-wj"],"summary":"The WJ's rear plastic bumper cover and factory spare tire position under the cargo floor are functional for street use but inadequate for trail builds. A steel rear bumper with swing-out carrier solves both problems simultaneously.","description":"The factory WJ stores the spare tire in a well under the cargo floor. Accessing it requires removing gear from the cargo area, opening the tailgate, pulling up the cargo floor, and then extracting the spare \u2014 a time-consuming process when you are already stuck on the trail and need to change a tire quickly. Moving the spare to a swing-out carrier on a rear bumper puts it at eye level, accessible in under a minute, without unloading the vehicle.\n\nA swing-out carrier also allows running a larger spare than the factory under-floor well permits. The WJ's factory spare is a 30\" donut or a limited-size full spare \u2014 with a swing-out, you can carry a full-size matching spare (33x12.5 or whatever you run) that can actually get you home in one piece if you sidewall a tire on sharp rock.\n\nThe WJ hatch opens independently of the swing-out on all well-designed aftermarket carriers \u2014 the swing arm pivots to the side and the hatch opens as normal. This preserves the daily-driver functionality of the rear cargo area.\n\n**Rock Hard 4x4 WJ rear bumper with swing-out ($850):** One of the few WJ-specific rear bumpers built for the subframe mounting geometry. Includes a 2\" receiver, integrated D-ring tabs, and a lockable swing-out carrier rated for a full-size spare up to 37\" diameter. The Rock Hard bumper is heavier (approximately 165 lbs with the carrier arm) \u2014 plan for a helper on installation.\n\n**Smittybilt SRC rear bumper ($480):** A functional mid-tier option without the swing-out carrier. If you do not need to carry a full-size spare externally, the Smittybilt is a solid choice. It provides a 2\" receiver and D-ring tabs at a lower cost. Add a separate swing-out carrier later if the need develops.\n\nThe WJ rear bumper mounts to the rear subframe crossmembers \u2014 the same unibody subframe that carries the rear suspension. The factory plastic bumper and impact absorber bolt to this structure. A steel rear bumper with a full-size swing-out spare adds 165\u2013200 lbs to the rear of the vehicle; confirm the subframe mounting bolts are clean and torqued correctly, and inspect the subframe for rust at the mounting points before installing heavy hardware.\n\nAfter installing, check that the hatch opens fully with the swing arm latched. Adjust the carrier arm stops if the hatch path is restricted. Check swing arm latch tension \u2014 it should hold firmly with no rattle on rough roads.\n\nA 2\" receiver integrated into the rear bumper is the correct rear recovery attachment for the WJ. Use a rated receiver-mount D-ring adapter (not a ball mount) and a 3/4\" shackle. Do not attach snatch straps or tow straps directly to the bumper structure \u2014 the shear forces are different from the designed mounting load. See recovery-points-wj for detailed recovery rigging guidance."},{"id":"rock-sliders-wj","title":"Rock Sliders for the WJ Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wj","category":"armor","subcategory":"sliders","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":750},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","sockets","torque wrench","drill"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["armor","rock sliders","rocker panels","unibody","protection","GenRight"],"parts":[{"name":"GenRight WJ rock sliders (tube style)","vendor":"GenRight Off Road","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Armor Works WJ rocker guards","vendor":"Armor Works","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Poison Spyder WJ sliders","vendor":"Poison Spyder Customs","price_estimate_usd":395}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Rock Slider Comparison \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"},{"title":"WJ Rocker Protection Guide \u2014 Pirate4x4","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com"}],"alternatives":["skid-plates-wj","front-bumper-wj"],"summary":"The WJ's rocker panels are structural members of the unibody \u2014 damage here is not cosmetic, it weakens the vehicle's integrity. Frame-mounted tube sliders that sit below the rocker are required protection for rock crawling. Step-style nerf bars do not qualify.","description":"Unlike a body-on-frame Jeep where the rocker panel is a body component that can be dented without structural consequence, the WJ's unibody rockers carry real load. Severe damage \u2014 a rock punching through the outer rocker skin and collapsing the inner structure \u2014 compromises torsional rigidity and can affect door fit. It also starts the rust cycle that is already the WJ's biggest structural enemy.\n\nA rock slider's job is to take the hit instead of the rocker. For this to work, the slider must be positioned below and outboard of the rocker panel, and it must be mounted to something strong enough to withstand the impact without transferring it to the body. On the WJ, that means mounting to the rear subframe crossmembers \u2014 the same structure that carries the rear suspension.\n\nThis point causes confusion in the WJ community. Step-style running boards and nerf bars that bolt to the body sills and sit at rocker height look like protection but are not. When a rock contacts a step bar that is mounted to the body, the force transmits directly to the unibody \u2014 you get the same impact as no protection at all, plus the risk of tearing the mounting hardware out of the body. A tube slider that mounts below the rocker line to the subframe acts as a structural sacrificial component.\n\nGenRight, Armor Works, and Poison Spyder all make WJ-specific sliders that mount correctly to the subframe structure. The GenRight sliders are a clean tube design that runs flush with the bottom of the rocker and extends below it \u2014 when the WJ leans on a rock, the slider contacts first and spreads the load to the subframe.\n\nThe WJ slider install requires removing the factory plastic body cladding at the rocker area to access the mounting points. The subframe crossmember mounting bolts penetrate through the unibody floor \u2014 torque them to spec and use thread-locking compound. The sliders should be firm with no movement when the vehicle is loaded or flexed; any rattle or movement indicates insufficient torque or a mounting point that needs inspection.\n\nAfter install, check ground clearance. WJ-specific tube sliders typically add 0\u20132\" to the overall vehicle width and reduce ground clearance at the sides by 1\u20132\". Confirm there is no interference with the rear tire at full droop if the slider extends aft of the rear door.\n\nBefore installing sliders on a high-mileage or northern-state WJ, do the rocker rust inspection described in rust-wj. Installing sliders over rotted unibody rockers gives you a false sense of security. If the rocker panels are compromised, address the rust first."},{"id":"skid-plates-wj","title":"Skid Plate Kit for the WJ Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wj","category":"armor","subcategory":"skid plates","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":700},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","sockets","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["armor","skid plates","oil pan","transfer case","fuel tank","NV247","protection"],"parts":[{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 WJ skid plate kit","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":485},{"name":"Skid Row Offroad WJ kit","vendor":"Skid Row Offroad","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Individual transfer case skid (WJ NV247)","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":145}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Grand Cherokee Skid Plate Review \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"}],"alternatives":["rock-sliders-wj","front-bumper-wj"],"summary":"The WJ's longer wheelbase relative to a Wrangler means more belly is exposed to rock strikes. A skid plate kit covering the oil pan, transmission, transfer case, and fuel tank is essential protection for anything beyond forest roads.","description":"The WJ's underbody has several vulnerable components that sit low and are expensive to damage or replace:\n\n**Oil pan:** The 4.0L's cast iron oil pan is more durable than aluminum, but a direct rock hit can still crack it or damage the drain plug area. On lifted WJs with a lower approach angle to the belly, the oil pan is an early rock contact point.\n\n**Transfer case:** The NV247 (Quadra-Drive II) transfer case is the most expensive component under the WJ and sits notably low. Replacing an NV247 with a used unit runs $400\u2013800 plus labor; a new unit is much more. Protecting it with steel is straightforward insurance.\n\n**Transmission:** The 45RFE (4.0L) or 545RFE (V8) transmission sits ahead of the transfer case. On aggressive rock crawling, the transmission pan and case can take strikes.\n\n**Fuel tank:** The WJ's fuel tank is exposed under the rear passenger compartment area. A rock puncture here is a fire risk and a serious trail emergency.\n\n**Rock Hard 4x4 WJ skid plate kit ($485):** A comprehensive 3/16\" steel kit covering oil pan, transmission, and transfer case as a single belly unit, with a separate fuel tank skid. Rock Hard designs to the WJ's specific mounting geometry \u2014 the plates bolt to factory brackets and crossmembers without cutting or drilling. The interconnected belly skid provides better protection than separate plates because it bridges gaps between components.\n\n**Skid Row Offroad WJ kit ($420):** Similar coverage and gauge, with individual plates for each component area. The modular approach lets you install in stages and replace individual plates if damaged without replacing the whole kit.\n\nIf budget requires prioritizing, install the transfer case skid first \u2014 it is the most exposed and most expensive component to replace.\n\nThe WJ skid plate install is primarily a bolt-on job if you have factory brackets intact. On a lifted WJ, confirm that the plates do not contact the driveshaft at full suspension travel \u2014 some kits require minor trimming or a spacer at the driveshaft clearance point. Recheck all mounting hardware after the first 500 miles; vibration from trail use can back out bolts.\n\nKeep skid plates clean of packed mud \u2014 heavy mud accumulation adds weight in an area you do not want it and can trap moisture against metal."},{"id":"lockers-wj","title":"Differential Locker Options \u2014 Front and Rear WJ Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wj","category":"axles","subcategory":"lockers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":1200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","sockets","torque wrench","bearing press","differential setup tools"],"skills_required":["differential service","bearing installation","basic axle work"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["locker","differential","Dana 30","Dana 44","ARB","ELocker","Powertrax","traction"],"parts":[{"name":"Powertrax Lock-Right (Dana 30 front, WJ)","vendor":"Powertrax","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"ARB Air Locker (Dana 30 front, RD100)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":550},{"name":"Eaton ELocker (Dana 44 rear, WJ)","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":680},{"name":"ARB Air Locker (Dana 44 rear, RD136)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":595},{"name":"ARB CKMA12 on-board air compressor","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Locker Guide \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"},{"title":"ARB Air Locker Installation Guide","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"}],"alternatives":["regear-wj","transfer-case-wj"],"summary":"The WJ's solid Dana 30 front and Dana 44 rear are both lockable \u2014 a genuine advantage over the IFS WK that followed. An ARB front and ELocker rear is the highest-traction WJ build, but a lunch-box locker front or rear is a meaningful upgrade on its own.","description":"The WJ's front Dana 30 is a solid axle with an open differential from the factory. This means any spin at a front wheel goes nowhere \u2014 the differential sends power to the spinning wheel, not the one with traction.\n\n**Powertrax Lock-Right ($280):** The budget-friendly \"lunch-box\" locker \u2014 it installs inside the open differential carrier without replacing the carrier itself. Install requires removing the differential cover, pulling the open carrier, disassembling it, pressing in the Lock-Right unit, and reassembling. No ring-and-pinion setup is required. The Lock-Right operates automatically \u2014 it locks on deceleration and unlocks slightly under drive torque. On-road behavior includes mild clunking in turns, which is normal. For trail use, it provides full lock-up and is significantly cheaper than air lockers.\n\n**ARB Air Locker (RD100, $550 + air supply):** A full selective locker \u2014 locked on command from a switch in the cab, open at all other times. Requires on-board air (ARB CKMA12 or similar) and a pneumatic line routed to the front axle. The air locker is the best of both worlds: open differential behavior on pavement, locked when you need it on the trail. Installation requires a professional differential setup with proper pattern checking \u2014 this is not a beginner install.\n\n**OX Locker ($575):** A cable-actuated selective locker with a different mechanism than the ARB. Similar selectability; requires a cable run to the cab. Less common than the ARB but well-regarded.\n\nThe WJ's Dana 44 rear (standard on Laredo, Limited, and all V8 models) is a more robust starting point than the D30 front. It accepts the same basic locker options plus the popular Eaton ELocker.\n\n**Eaton ELocker ($680):** An electronic locker actuated by a 12V solenoid. Lock and unlock from a cab switch; full lock engagement on command. The ELocker requires a wiring harness run to the rear axle but no air system. For trail use, it is as effective as the ARB but simpler to install and maintain.\n\n**ARB Air Locker (RD136, $595):** If you are already running an ARB front and have on-board air, a second line to the rear ARB requires no additional air source. A dual-ARB setup is the most capable configuration for a competition rock crawling WJ.\n\n**Powertrax Lock-Right rear ($280):** Installs in the D44 the same way as the front unit. Less desirable in the rear for a vehicle that also sees street use because locked rear behavior is more noticeable on pavement than locked front, but for a dedicated trail rig it is an inexpensive improvement.\n\nSome WJ models have the Quadra-Drive II system with Vari-Lok differentials \u2014 these are active clutch-pack units in the front and rear differentials that transfer torque based on slip. They are not true lockers and wear their clutch packs over time. The Vari-Lok can be supplemented with real lockers in each axle \u2014 replacing the Vari-Lok unit with an ARB or ELocker in the Dana 44 rear is a common upgrade on high-mileage Quadra-Drive WJs whose Vari-Lok is worn."},{"id":"regear-wj","title":"Re-Gearing the WJ Grand Cherokee for Larger Tires","vehicle":"wj","category":"axles","subcategory":"gearing","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":600,"high":1400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","axle dial indicator","bearing press","torque wrench","ring and pinion setup tools"],"skills_required":["differential setup","ring and pinion installation","backlash measurement"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["regear","ring and pinion","Dana 44","4.10","4.56","tire size","gearing"],"parts":[{"name":"4.10 ring and pinion (Dana 44 rear WJ)","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":185},{"name":"4.56 ring and pinion (Dana 44 rear WJ)","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":195},{"name":"4.10 ring and pinion (Dana 30 front WJ)","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":175},{"name":"Master overhaul kit (Dana 44)","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":145},{"name":"Master overhaul kit (Dana 30)","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":125}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Regear Guide \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"},{"title":"Gear Ratio Calculator \u2014 Crawlpedia","url":"https://www.crawlpedia.com"}],"alternatives":["lockers-wj","tire-sizing-wj"],"summary":"Going from 30\" tires to 33s or 35s without re-gearing leaves significant engine RPM on the table \u2014 the WJ will feel sluggish, the transmission will hunt gears, and fuel economy drops. Match the gears to the tire size and get the ratio right on both axles.","description":"The WJ's powertrain was designed around specific tire sizes. The 4.0L with factory 3.55 gears and 30\" tires gives a specific effective gear ratio at the wheels. Swap in 33\" tires and the effective ratio drops \u2014 the engine is now turning fewer revolutions per mile of forward motion than it was designed to. The result: the WJ pulls harder than expected to get moving, the transmission shifts to a lower gear more aggressively to compensate, and fuel economy gets worse, not better.\n\nRe-gearing restores the intended ratio. It does not add horsepower \u2014 it makes the power available in the RPM range where the engine is most efficient.\n\n**Factory WJ gearing:** Most WJ models left the factory with 3.55:1 gears. Some V8 tow-package models received 3.73:1. Check the door sticker (which may list the axle ratio) or count the ring gear teeth divided by the pinion teeth to confirm.\n\n**For 33\" tires:** 4.10:1 gears. This is the most common regear on the WJ and restores near-factory driving behavior on 33s. The WJ is noticeably more responsive and the transmission stops hunting at highway speeds.\n\n**For 35\" tires:** 4.56:1 gears. A 35-tire WJ with 4.10s is better than 3.55s but still somewhat over-geared. 4.56 is the right call at 35\".\n\n**Front and rear must match.** The WJ's transfer case splits power front to rear \u2014 mismatched gears create binding in the drivetrain that wears transfer case chains and differentials rapidly. Order both axles' gears at the same time.\n\nThe WJ Dana 44 rear is the standard on Laredo, Limited, and all V8 models. The Chrysler 9.25 rear is found on some early V8 (5.2L) models. These axles are NOT the same and their gears are not interchangeable. Before ordering parts, identify your rear axle:\n\n- Count the cover bolts. The Dana 44 cover has 10 bolts and is roughly shaped like a D. The C9.25 cover has 12 bolts and is more oval.\n- The C9.25 uses different gears and a different gear set family than the Dana 44 \u2014 availability is more limited.\n\nThe Dana 44 rear gear sets are widely available from Yukon Gear, G2 Axle, and Richmond. Both 4.10 and 4.56 ratios are well-stocked items.\n\nRing and pinion setup requires precise backlash and bearing preload measurement. The dial indicator setup, pattern check, and correct shimming are not beginner work. A shop experienced with Dana axles will run $350\u2013550 per axle for the labor on a regear. Budget $800\u20131,200 total for a front and rear regear with quality gears and a good shop. A poorly set ring and pinion will whine constantly and fail prematurely \u2014 the extra cost of professional setup is not where to cut corners."},{"id":"rust-wj","title":"Rust Inspection and Repair on the WJ Grand Cherokee Unibody","vehicle":"wj","category":"body","subcategory":"rust","type":"reference","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":50},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":5000},"tools_required":["screwdriver (flathead)","wire brush","angle grinder","MIG welder","rust converter","jack stands","floor jack"],"skills_required":["rust assessment","MIG welding","body work basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["rust","unibody","structural","safety","body","rocker panels","floor pan","frame horns"],"parts":[{"name":"Rocker panel patch panels (WJ)","vendor":"Raybuck Auto Body Parts","price_estimate_usd":140},{"name":"Rust converter (POR-15 Starter Kit)","vendor":"POR-15","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Weld-through primer","vendor":"SEM","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Fluid Film (internal cavity protection)","vendor":"Fluid Film","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Eastwood Rust Encapsulator","vendor":"Eastwood","price_estimate_usd":35}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Grand Cherokee Rust Repair \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"},{"title":"Unibody Rust Structural Safety \u2014 Pirate4x4","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com"}],"alternatives":["rock-sliders-wj","front-bumper-wj"],"summary":"The WJ Grand Cherokee is famous in rust-belt communities for rotted rocker panels. These are not cosmetic repairs \u2014 the unibody rockers carry structural load. A WJ with severely rusted rockers is not safe to drive, and installing armor or sliders over rotten rockers gives false confidence without actual protection.","description":"The WJ's unibody design channels road spray and salt into the rocker panel cavity where it sits and corrodes from the inside out. The outer skin may look acceptable while the inner structure is completely gone. This is the specific failure mode that makes the WJ notorious among rust-belt buyers: the rust looks minor from the outside and catastrophic from underneath.\n\nRocker panel rust on the WJ is a structural issue, not a cosmetic one. The rockers are load-bearing members of the unibody \u2014 they resist the twisting forces from the suspension and carry some of the cab's bending loads. A WJ with both inner and outer rocker skins gone along most of the length has significantly reduced structural integrity. Door misalignment is the early visible symptom; beyond that, the floor pan edges and B-pillar connections begin to separate.\n\nDo the inspection with the vehicle on jack stands at a comfortable working height, with a strong flashlight and a sharp flathead screwdriver.\n\n**Outer rocker panel:** Poke the screwdriver firmly into the lower edge of the rocker at 4\u20136 inch intervals. Sound steel resists with a solid knock; rusted steel gives under the screwdriver tip with little force. If the outer skin perforates easily, assume the inner skin is worse.\n\n**Inner rocker at floor pan seam:** Pull back the carpet and floor mats inside the vehicle. The inner rocker meets the floor pan along a seam approximately 4\" from the base of the door threshold. Probe this seam from inside. Perforations or soft spots here indicate structural rust.\n\n**Front frame horns:** The forward extensions of the unibody at the bumper mounting area. These carry bumper and winch loads. Probe with a screwdriver. Frame horns with severe rust are not safe for winch or recovery hardware.\n\n**Rear spare tire well:** The recessed floor section in the cargo area collects standing water. Check the perimeter and the flat sections for perforations.\n\nThis is the honest assessment the WJ community often does not want to hear: a WJ with both inner and outer rocker skins completely missing along more than 18\u201324 inches on either side is an economically difficult repair that requires significant fabrication time and structural expertise. A professional rust repair shop doing the job correctly \u2014 cutting to sound metal, fitting welded patches, sealing and coating \u2014 may quote $2,000\u20135,000 for severe bilateral rocker repair. Compare that to the vehicle's value.\n\nGet a professional quote before investing in any other WJ modifications if rocker rust is present. The worst outcome is a well-modified WJ sitting on structurally compromised rockers.\n\n**Surface rust to minor perforation (under 4\"):** Grind to bare metal, treat with POR-15 or Ospho rust converter, apply weld-through primer, weld in patch panels using 1\" stitch welds, coat with Eastwood Rust Encapsulator.\n\n**Moderate rocker rust (perforated sections up to 18\"):** Patch panels from Raybuck are WJ-specific and reduce fitting time. Cut back to solid metal \u2014 1\u20132\" past the visible rust line into healthy steel. Fit and weld patches. This is a MIG welding job with proper penetration \u2014 do not use filler or epoxy over perforated structural metal.\n\n**Internal cavity treatment:** After any rocker repair, spray the internal cavity with Fluid Film or a similar cavity wax through any accessible hole or through the body plug holes. Annual cavity wax application on any WJ used in wet climates is a maintenance item worth doing."},{"id":"wj-front-pads-rotors-wj","title":"Front Brake Pad and Rotor Replacement","vehicle":"wj","category":"brakes","subcategory":"service","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":2.5},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":220},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","19mm socket (lug nuts)","13mm socket","15mm socket","torque wrench","C-clamp or piston tool","brake cleaner"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["brakes","safety_critical","rotors","pads"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar front rotor (each)","part_number":"52098672","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Mopar front pad kit","part_number":"5093183AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Centric Premium rotor (aftermarket)","part_number":"121.67042","vendor":"Centric / PartsGeek","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Akebono ProACT ceramic pads","vendor":"Akebono","price_estimate_usd":55}],"citations":[{"title":"iFixit \u2014 1999\u20132004 WJ Brake Pad Replacement","url":"https://www.ifixit.com/Guide/1999+2000+2001+2002+2003+2004+Jeep+Grand+Cherokee+WJ+Brake+Pad+Replacement/77670"},{"title":"JeepSpecs \u2014 WJ Brakes Reference","url":"https://wjjeeps.com/brakes.htm"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Replace pads and rotors as a set on a vehicle this old \u2014 a $35 aftermarket rotor and $40 pads gets you new everything for about an hour of work per side. Front rotor is 305mm \u00d7 26mm vented, and the caliper bracket bolts torque to 66\u201385 ft-lbs.","description":"This is the most common DIY job on the WJ and the one that absolutely has to be done correctly \u2014 brakes are safety-critical, period. The factory rotor is undersized for the truck's weight, and pad life on stock pads is 30\u201340k miles. By the time you're doing this job, the rotors are usually scored and warped enough to make resurfacing pointless. Buy new rotors.\n\nA note on caliper history: WJs built **through May 10, 2002** use Teves calipers. WJs built **after May 11, 2002** use Akebono \"fully-closed\" style calipers. The pad and bracket geometry is different between the two. If you're replacing a caliper, check the build date plate on the driver door jamb to be sure you get the right one. Pads are largely interchangeable between the two but kit number 5093183AA is the revised pad that fits both.\n\nLoosen the lug nuts (19mm) before lifting. Jack the front, set on stands, pull wheels. Behind the rotor, the caliper bracket has two big 15mm bolts (some are 18mm \u2014 check yours). Pull the whole caliper-and-bracket assembly off as one unit \u2014 hang it from the coil spring with a bungee, never let it dangle on the rubber line. The rotor slides off the hub once the caliper is out of the way (sometimes it needs a few hammer taps if it's rust-welded).\n\nPush the piston back into the caliper with a C-clamp \u2014 open the bleeder first so the old fluid pushes out instead of back up the line. Slide the new pads into the bracket, drop the bracket back over the new rotor, torque the bracket bolts to 76 ft-lbs (middle of the 66\u201385 range), torque the slide pins to 25 ft-lbs. Wheel on, lug nuts to 95 ft-lbs.\n\nBefore driving \u2014 pump the brake pedal multiple times to push the piston back out against the new pads. The first pedal will go to the floor if you skip this. Bed the pads in with 8\u201310 moderate stops from 35 mph, then let them cool.","steps":["Loosen lug nuts.","Lift front, set on stands, remove wheels.","Remove the two caliper bracket bolts (15mm or 18mm).","Lift the caliper assembly off, support with a bungee \u2014 do not let it hang on the brake hose.","Pull the rotor off the hub.","Open the bleeder, push the caliper piston back with a C-clamp.","Swap pads in the caliper bracket.","Install new rotor on hub.","Reinstall caliper assembly. Torque bracket bolts to 76 ft-lbs, slide pins to 25 ft-lbs.","Reinstall wheels, torque lugs to 95 ft-lbs.","Pump the brake pedal 10+ times until firm.","Bed pads with 8\u201310 moderate stops from 35 mph."],"pros":["$90 in parts on the cheap end, $220 with Mopar","Pads on Akebono-style calipers wear evenly when set up correctly","Restores stopping distance after a winter on cheap pads"],"cons":["Stuck rotors on rust-belt trucks are a fight","Two caliper styles (Teves pre-2002 vs Akebono post-2002) \u2014 check before buying","Skipping the bed-in procedure causes pulsing and noise"]},{"id":"sye-wj","title":"Slip Yoke Eliminator and CV Rear Driveshaft \u2014 WJ Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wj","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"SYE","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":530,"high":700},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","sockets","torque wrench","snap ring pliers","bearing press","measuring tape"],"skills_required":["drivetrain R&R","transfer case removal"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["SYE","slip yoke eliminator","CV driveshaft","lift","vibration","NV242","driveline"],"parts":[{"name":"BYP SYE kit for NV242 (WJ)","vendor":"BYP Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":275},{"name":"JB Conversions SYE kit (NV242)","vendor":"JB Conversions","price_estimate_usd":285},{"name":"Tom Wood's CV rear driveshaft (WJ NV242 SYE)","vendor":"Tom Wood's Custom Drive Shafts","price_estimate_usd":295},{"name":"Adams Driveshafts WJ rear CV driveshaft","vendor":"Adams Driveshafts","price_estimate_usd":310}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ SYE Install Guide \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"},{"title":"Tom Wood's Driveshafts WJ Measurement Guide","url":"https://www.tomwoodscustomdriveshafts.com"}],"alternatives":["transfer-case-wj","wj-lift-kit-guide"],"summary":"Lift the WJ more than 3 inches without addressing the rear driveline and you will have highway vibration and a rear driveshaft that can exit the transfer case at full suspension droop. The SYE and CV driveshaft fix both problems and are a required companion to any serious lift.","description":"The WJ's NV242 and NV247 transfer cases use a slip yoke rear output. The rear driveshaft slides onto the output shaft and uses the slip to accommodate the small length changes as the suspension cycles. At factory ride height (and with factory suspension travel), the slip yoke works correctly and the driveshaft angle is within acceptable limits.\n\nAdd 3\" or more of lift and two problems emerge. First, the driveshaft angle steepens significantly \u2014 the transfer case now sits higher relative to the rear differential, creating an acute U-joint angle that causes vibration at highway speed and accelerated U-joint wear. Second, with extended suspension travel from lift and longer shocks, the driveshaft can reach full slip yoke extension at maximum droop \u2014 meaning the shaft leaves the transfer case on the trail. This is a serious failure mode: a driveshaft departure on a trail can damage the driveshaft, the transfer case output, and the surrounding underbody.\n\nA Slip Yoke Eliminator converts the transfer case rear output from a slip yoke to a fixed flanged output. The BYP Manufacturing and JB Conversions SYE kits for the NV242 are the most common choices \u2014 both replace the slip yoke and housing with a machined aluminum housing and flanged output stub shaft. Installation requires removing the transfer case, disassembling the rear output housing, and pressing in the new shaft and housing.\n\nAfter the SYE, the factory driveshaft will not fit \u2014 the output is now a flange, not a slip yoke, and the length is different. A custom CV rear driveshaft from Tom Wood's or Adams Driveshafts is required. Both companies take measurements and build to spec. **Measure your specific transfer case output flange to rear axle flange distance with the suspension at normal ride height before ordering** \u2014 provide these measurements to the driveshaft company. The CV driveshaft handles the angle change that the slip yoke previously provided; it is lighter than a standard driveshaft and effectively immune to the vibration issues.\n\nThe NV247 also uses a slip yoke rear output and has the same fundamental problem at lift. SYE kits for the NV247 are less common and more expensive than for the NV242. BYP and JB Conversions both list NV247 SYE options \u2014 confirm compatibility before ordering. Some WJ owners with the NV247 swap it for an NV242 (a simpler, more supported unit) as part of the lift build; a used NV242 runs $280\u2013400 and the swap is well-documented.\n\nIf you are planning a lift and have not done the SYE, do both in the same build sequence. Installing a lift, driving on it, then adding the SYE means pulling things apart twice. Time the SYE with the lift install."},{"id":"transfer-case-wj","title":"Transfer Case Service \u2014 NV242 and NV247 in the WJ","vehicle":"wj","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"transfer case","type":"reference","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":50,"high":400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","sockets","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic drivetrain service"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["transfer case","NV242","NV247","Quadra-Drive II","chain","drivetrain","service"],"parts":[{"name":"ATF+4 fluid (1 qt)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"NV247 chain replacement kit","vendor":"Novak Conversions","price_estimate_usd":190},{"name":"NV242 rebuilt transfer case (exchange)","vendor":"Various","price_estimate_usd":380}],"citations":[{"title":"NV242 and NV247 Transfer Case Guide \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"},{"title":"WJ NV247 Chain Service \u2014 JeepForum","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com"}],"alternatives":["sye-wj","transfer-case-fluid-wj"],"summary":"The WJ uses two transfer cases \u2014 the relatively simple NV242 (SelectTrac) or the full-time NV247 (Quadra-Drive II). Know which you have, service the fluid on schedule, and watch the NV247 for chain wear on any vehicle over 100,000 miles.","description":"The NV242 (SelectTrac) is found in most WJ models. It is a combination part-time/full-time unit with a floor shifter or electronic selector. Positions: 2H, 4H Part-Time, 4H Full-Time, Neutral, 4L. The 2H position is rear-wheel drive only; the two 4H positions differ in whether the center differential is locked (Part-Time) or open (Full-Time). The NV242 can be operated in 2H for street driving \u2014 it is a true part-time option.\n\nThe NV247 (Quadra-Drive II) is the full-time 4WD unit. It has no 2WD position \u2014 it is always distributing power to both axles. The selector has 4H, Neutral, and 4L. The NV247 uses a planetary differential in the center section with Vari-Lok clutch packs. On pavement, it behaves like an open center differential and does not bind. Off the trail, the Vari-Lok clutches engage to transfer torque to the axle with traction.\n\nCheck the shifter position labels, or look at the VIN door sticker (the axle/transfer case code is listed there). The NV247 is found on Overland and some Limited trims.\n\nThe NV247's internal chain transfers power from the planetary unit to the rear output shaft. Over high mileage (commonly 120,000+ miles), this chain stretches. The symptom is a distinct rattling or chain-slap noise under light throttle loads or during coast-down in 4WD. The noise is often intermittent at first and becomes constant as wear progresses.\n\nLeft unaddressed, a worn NV247 chain can damage the case housing or break under load \u2014 a trail breakdown. The repair requires removing the transfer case and disassembling the planetary unit to access the chain. Novak Conversions and other suppliers sell chain replacement kits ($185\u2013190) with new chain and wear guides. This is a shop job; proper assembly requires care with the planetary unit.\n\nIf you are purchasing a high-mileage WJ with the NV247, ask the seller to drive it in 4WD while you listen from the passenger seat. Chain slap is audible and confirms a needed repair.\n\nBoth the NV242 and NV247 use **ATF+4** fluid \u2014 not gear oil, not generic ATF. Mopar ATF+4 or a compatible licensed equivalent (not all ATF fluids are ATF+4 compatible). The fill capacity is approximately 1.1 quarts for the NV242 and 1.4 quarts for the NV247.\n\nService interval: every 30,000 miles under normal use. If the WJ crosses water regularly or is used in dusty/muddy conditions, drop to 15,000 miles. The drain plug is on the bottom of the case; the fill plug is on the side. Fill to the level of the fill hole.\n\nNote: the NV247 cannot be run dry \u2014 it requires fluid at all times for the planetary differential to function. A leaking NV247 output seal that goes unaddressed will eventually run the case low enough to cause planetary damage."},{"id":"aux-lighting-wj","title":"Auxiliary Lighting for the WJ Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wj","category":"electrical","subcategory":"lighting","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":700},"tools_required":["drill","wire cutters","crimper","sockets","multimeter"],"skills_required":["basic electrical","wiring relay circuits"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["lighting","auxiliary lights","LED","light bar","Baja Designs","wiring","relay"],"parts":[{"name":"Baja Designs Squadron Pro pod lights (pair)","vendor":"Baja Designs","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Rigid Industries 40\" E-Series light bar","vendor":"Rigid Industries","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Nilight 40\" LED light bar (budget)","vendor":"Nilight","price_estimate_usd":85},{"name":"Relay wiring harness with switch (universal)","vendor":"Nilight or KC HiLites","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Add-a-circuit tap (for fuse box)","vendor":"Littelfuse","price_estimate_usd":8}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Aux Light Wiring Guide \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"}],"alternatives":["dual-battery-wj","winch-wiring-wj"],"summary":"The WJ's factory headlights are adequate for highway driving but fall short on dark, narrow trails where you need to see 200+ feet ahead. A quality 40\\\" roof bar or a pair of A-pillar pod lights gives the illumination needed for night trail use without overloading the factory wiring.","description":"The WJ's long hood and flat roof make it well-suited for a roof-mounted light bar. A 40\" bar mounted at the roofline illuminates far ahead \u2014 useful for forest roads and moderate trails where the bar stays above brush contact. On technical rock trails, a roof bar can be problematic because it gets hit by overhanging terrain, but for most trail use it is the most practical choice.\n\nA-pillar pod mounts are the alternative for technical rock use \u2014 the pods sit low on the A-pillars, illuminating directly forward and slightly downward at the bumper. Baja Designs Squadron Pro pods are the benchmark quality option at $320 per pair; they produce an intense, controlled spot beam that is extremely effective at distances where the factory headlights drop off. Pod mounts require fabricated or purchased brackets for the WJ's A-pillars \u2014 check availability from the manufacturer or plan to fabricate 1/4\" steel brackets.\n\nNever wire auxiliary lights directly from the fuse box to the lights without a relay. A relay allows a small-current switch circuit to control a higher-current load. The relay draws its power directly from the battery (or from a dedicated fused tap near the battery) and triggers when the switch circuit is activated. This prevents the switch, fuse box tap, and switch wiring from carrying the full amp load of the lights.\n\nA 40\" LED light bar draws approximately 8\u201312 amps at 12V. A Baja Designs Squadron Pro pair draws about 5\u20136 amps total. Either load is manageable for a properly fused add-a-circuit tap in the WJ fuse box (for the switch side) and a relay handling the load side. Use a 20-amp fuse on the load circuit for a 40\" bar.\n\nA 40\" light bar mounts to the roof either via a raised bracket off the front of the roof rack or in front of the roof itself on custom mounts. Drilling through the WJ roof for flush-mount brackets is the clean option but requires proper sealing at every penetration \u2014 use marine-grade sealant and stainless hardware. A rack-top mount that does not penetrate the roof is cleaner for resale and eliminates leak risk.\n\nRoute the wiring from the light bar down the A-pillar (tucked under the headliner and trim panel), through the firewall via an existing grommet, and to the relay under the hood. Keep the entire exterior run in split loom conduit to protect against UV and abrasion.\n\nCheck your garage clearance. A WJ with a light bar mounted on roof rail brackets adds 4\u20136\" of height at the front roofline. Mark the garage entry height with tape on the door frame before building any habit of driving in without thinking about it."},{"id":"dual-battery-wj","title":"Dual Battery Setup for the WJ Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wj","category":"electrical","subcategory":"dual battery","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":650},"tools_required":["drill","sockets","wire cutters","crimper","multimeter"],"skills_required":["basic electrical","battery installation"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["electrical","dual battery","secondary battery","DC-DC charger","Redarc","overland","AGM"],"parts":[{"name":"Northstar NSB-AGM31 battery (secondary)","vendor":"Northstar","price_estimate_usd":195},{"name":"Optima Yellow Top D34 (secondary)","vendor":"Optima","price_estimate_usd":215},{"name":"Redarc BCDC1220 DC-DC charger","vendor":"Redarc","price_estimate_usd":285},{"name":"CTEK D250SE DC-DC charger","vendor":"CTEK","price_estimate_usd":195},{"name":"Battery tray bracket (cargo area)","vendor":"Custom or universal","price_estimate_usd":65}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Dual Battery Overland Build \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"},{"title":"Redarc BCDC1220 Installation Guide","vendor":"Redarc","url":"https://www.redarc.com.au"}],"alternatives":["winch-wiring-wj","aux-lighting-wj"],"summary":"A simple battery isolator will charge a secondary battery but doesn't charge it properly. A DC-DC charger (Redarc, CTEK) is the correct solution \u2014 it provides a staged charge profile that extends battery life and can charge from either the alternator or solar panels.","description":"A relay-based battery isolator connects the secondary battery to the alternator circuit when the alternator is producing voltage. It works, but it charges the secondary battery at whatever voltage the alternator outputs \u2014 typically a bulk charge voltage without a proper absorption or float stage. Sealed AGM batteries charged this way over time can overcharge, reducing their capacity.\n\nA DC-DC charger (also called a DC-to-DC charger or battery-to-battery charger) takes voltage from the alternator circuit, steps it up to the correct charging voltage for the secondary battery, and applies a proper 3-stage charge profile. The Redarc BCDC1220 and CTEK D250SE are the most commonly used units for this application. They are also solar-input capable \u2014 add a roof-mounted panel and the secondary battery charges from the sun when parked.\n\nThe WJ cargo area has room for a Group 31 or Group 34 AGM battery mounted in a custom bracket bolted to the cargo floor. A Northstar NSB-AGM31 provides 100Ah of usable capacity \u2014 enough to run a 40W compressor fridge for 24\u201336 hours between charges. An Optima Yellow Top D34 is a smaller, lighter alternative at around 55Ah.\n\nThe battery must be secured in a metal bracket bolted to the floor \u2014 a loose battery in the cargo area is a hazard. Use a proper battery hold-down strap rated for automotive use, not zip ties. Fuse the cable from the DC-DC charger to the secondary battery within 18\" of the battery's positive terminal.\n\nThe DC-DC charger has three connections: input positive (from the starting battery or alternator), input negative (chassis ground), and output positive and negative to the secondary battery. Route the input cable through the firewall to the main battery; size this cable for the charger's maximum input current (the Redarc BCDC1220 draws about 25A input).\n\nKeep the starting battery circuit and secondary battery circuit isolated \u2014 the DC-DC charger is the only connection between them. This means the secondary battery cannot accidentally drain the starting battery. A fully dead secondary battery will not prevent the engine from starting.\n\nA 100Ah AGM secondary battery running a 40-watt compressor fridge can maintain operating temperature for roughly 30\u201340 hours on battery alone. With the alternator running (driving), the DC-DC charger maintains the secondary at near full charge indefinitely. Add a 100W solar panel and the secondary battery stays charged during extended camp time even without running the engine.\n\nTypical overland loads for a WJ: 12V fridge (35\u201345W average), USB charging (5\u201315W), LED camp lighting (10\u201325W), HAM radio receive mode (5\u20138W). Total draw of around 60\u201380W is well within a 100Ah AGM's capacity for 24-hour use."},{"id":"winch-wiring-wj","title":"Winch Electrical Installation \u2014 WJ Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wj","category":"electrical","subcategory":"winch","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":200},"tools_required":["wire cutters","crimper","multimeter","heat shrink gun","drill","sockets"],"skills_required":["basic electrical","wire routing","fusing"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["electrical","winch","wiring","battery","alternator","fusing","2/0 AWG"],"parts":[{"name":"2/0 AWG welding cable (15 ft)","vendor":"Various electrical supply","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"200A ANL fuse holder","vendor":"Blue Sea Systems","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"200A ANL fuse","vendor":"Blue Sea Systems","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"3/8\" terminal lugs (set)","vendor":"Ancor Marine","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Heat shrink connectors (assorted)","vendor":"Ancor","price_estimate_usd":14}],"citations":[{"title":"Winch Wiring Guide \u2014 WarnIndustries.com","url":"https://www.warnindustries.com"},{"title":"WJ Winch Install Thread \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"}],"alternatives":["winch-wj","dual-battery-wj"],"summary":"The WJ's factory wiring is not sized to power a winch. Running undersized wire from the battery to a 10,000 lb winch motor produces voltage drop, heat buildup, and slow winch speeds exactly when you need full power. Do the wiring right: 2/0 AWG cable, 200A fuse, correct terminals.","description":"The WJ 4.0L comes with a 90-amp alternator \u2014 adequate for normal accessories but not for sustained winching. A 10,000 lb winch motor draws 80\u2013130 amps at full load. This means the winch is pulling power from the battery, not the alternator, under heavy load. Running the winch in long pulls without the engine at moderate RPM will drain the battery.\n\nPractical implication: when winching, run the engine at 1,500\u20132,000 RPM rather than idle. This maximizes alternator output. A secondary battery (see dual-battery-wj) extends the reserve for longer recoveries. An upgraded 140A or 160A alternator is available for the WJ 4.0L from Powermaster and others \u2014 worthwhile if the WJ is regularly used for self-recovery in remote terrain.\n\nThe winch motor's peak current draw (up to 450A during stall) exceeds anything the factory wiring can handle. The winch must have its own dedicated circuit direct from the battery. The cable size determines how much resistance (and heat) the circuit generates.\n\n**Use 2/0 AWG (two-ought) welding cable** for runs up to 10 feet. For runs over 10 feet, step up to 4/0 AWG. Welding cable is more flexible than automotive battery cable and easier to route through tight spaces. Use proper crimped and heat-shrunk ring terminals \u2014 not wire nut connectors. Poor connections in a high-current circuit create resistance points that get hot and can melt wiring.\n\nRoute the positive cable from the battery positive terminal, through a 200A ANL fuse holder positioned within 18 inches of the battery, and forward to the winch. The negative cable runs from the winch directly to a battery negative terminal or a clean chassis ground near the battery \u2014 not through a fuse.\n\nA 200-amp ANL fuse is the standard protection for a 10,000\u201312,000 lb winch on the WJ's wiring gauge. The fuse should be rated at approximately 1.5x the winch's continuous current draw. Place the fuse holder within 18 inches of the battery positive terminal \u2014 this ensures the entire cable run is protected against a short.\n\nBlue Sea Systems ANL fuse holders are the quality standard for this application. Avoid cheap automotive fuse holders \u2014 at 200A under load, a poor contact in the fuse holder generates heat and resistance.\n\nRoute cables through the firewall using an existing grommet or a new weatherproof pass-through. Avoid routing near hot exhaust components. Secure the cable every 12\u201318 inches with cable clamps or zip ties to prevent chafing. The front bumper winch should have a short, direct cable run \u2014 the WJ's engine bay gives a reasonably direct path from the battery through the front bumper.\n\nAfter installation, test the winch under load with a voltage meter at the battery. Healthy voltage under load should stay above 12V while the engine is running. Below 11.5V at the battery under full winch load indicates a wiring or connection issue."},{"id":"wj-47-cooling-common-problems","title":"4.7L Common Problems: Cooling and Longevity","vehicle":"wj","category":"engine","subcategory":"common_problems","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":50,"high":900},"tools_required":["basic hand tools","coolant tester","OBD-II scanner"],"skills_required":["maintenance discipline","cooling-system basics"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2001","2002-2004"],"tags":["engine","cooling","maintenance","common-problems"],"parts":[{"name":"Radiator (heavy-duty)","vendor":"Mishimoto/CSF/Mopar","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Thermostat + housing","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Full-synthetic oil + filter (service)","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":55}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ maintenance schedules \u2014 JeepSpecs","url":"https://jeepspecs.com/wj-generation/specifications/maintenance-information-and-schedules/"},{"title":"Grand Cherokee WJ technical reference","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeep_Grand_Cherokee_(WJ)"}],"alternatives":["wj-death-wobble"],"summary":"The WJ's 4.7L PowerTech V8 is a strong engine with one rule: it lives or dies by its cooling system and its oil changes. Owners who keep the radiator healthy and run a strict synthetic-oil interval get 200,000-plus miles. Owners who let it overheat or sludge up crack a cylinder head. The 4.0L inline-six in the other WJs is the more forgiving engine.","description":"The honest verdict on the 4.7L: it is not a fragile engine, but it is an unforgiving one about heat and oil. The factory radiator and plastic end tanks degrade with age, and a marginal cooling system that an iron-block engine would shrug off will warp or crack a 4.7L head. If your WJ runs hot in traffic or climbing grades, treat it as urgent \u2014 replace the radiator with a quality unit, run a fresh thermostat, and burp the air out of the system properly (the WJ traps air and will read false-normal at the gauge while a pocket cooks the head).\n\nOil is the other half. The 4.7L is prone to sludge if it sees extended intervals or cheap oil; sludge starves the timing-chain tensioners and the heads. Run full synthetic on a disciplined interval and the engine simply does not develop the problem. While you are in the engine bay, the 4.0L-six WJs are far less demanding \u2014 the six's weak points are the usual radiator, the crank position sensor, and the exhaust manifold, not catastrophic overheating.\n\nAcross both engines, keep the cooling system fresh: coolant every few years, a healthy fan clutch, and a cap that holds pressure. A WJ that never overheats is a WJ that runs a long time.","steps":["Confirm the engine: 4.7L V8 vs 4.0L I6 \u2014 the 4.7L needs the strictest cooling/oil care.","Pressure-test the cooling system; replace an aged radiator and thermostat before failure.","Burp trapped air after any coolant work; verify the fan clutch and pressure cap.","Put the 4.7L on a strict full-synthetic oil interval to prevent sludge.","Service coolant on schedule and address overheating the day it starts."]},{"id":"seats-wj","title":"Seat Upgrades for the WJ Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wj","category":"interior","subcategory":"seats","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":900},"tools_required":["sockets","torque wrench","drill","tape measure"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","interior R&R"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["seats","interior","Corbeau","Mastercraft","bucket seats","overland"],"parts":[{"name":"Corbeau LG1 reclining bucket seat","vendor":"Corbeau","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Corbeau A4 fixed-back racing seat","vendor":"Corbeau","price_estimate_usd":340},{"name":"Mastercraft Baja RS seat","vendor":"Mastercraft","price_estimate_usd":485},{"name":"Summit Fabrication WJ seat bracket","vendor":"Summit Fabrication","price_estimate_usd":145}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Seat Swap Thread \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The WJ's front seats are comfortable enough for daily driving but wear quickly under trail use \u2014 the bolster areas compress, the foam loses support, and the cloth tears at high-stress points. A quality aftermarket bucket seat is a genuine improvement for both on-trail control and long-distance overland comfort.","description":"The WJ Limited and Overland trims came with genuine leather seating. Leather in good condition ages reasonably well and provides decent support. If you have a Limited with intact leather seats, there may be no compelling reason to replace them unless you want more lateral bolstering.\n\nThe base Laredo and Sport trims used cloth seats that develop problems by 100,000+ miles: foam compression at the seat cushion center and driver's bolster, seam separation at the high-stress corners, and in some cases fabric tearing at the shoulder bolster. On the trail, a seat with minimal side bolstering leaves you bracing against the door on every off-camber section.\n\nThe WJ's front seats mount to the floor via factory track rails. For an aftermarket seat, the same two options apply as other WJ-era vehicles: adapt the factory tracks with a seat-specific adapter, or replace the tracks with aftermarket seat rails.\n\nSummit Fabrication makes WJ-specific brackets that mate common aftermarket seats to the factory mounting points while maintaining fore-aft adjustment. These are the clean solution. Universal Corbeau or Sparco rails work as a lower-cost alternative but require measuring and drilling to achieve correct seat height and angle.\n\nThe WJ's front headroom is adequate for most seats, but measure the distance from the floor to the headliner before ordering a high-back seat. A seat with a tall headrest can contact the headliner in a stock or minimally-lifted WJ.\n\n**Corbeau LG1 ($280):** The right choice for a WJ that splits time between trail use and street driving. The LG1 is a full reclining bucket with serious side bolstering, a low enough profile to work in the WJ cab, and compatibility with the factory lap/shoulder belt system. Easy to get in and out of for daily use.\n\n**Corbeau A4 ($340):** A fixed-back seat with more aggressive bolstering for dedicated trail and overland use. Does not recline \u2014 less convenient if the WJ is used daily. The extra lateral support is worth the trade-off on long overland trips or serious rock crawling.\n\n**Mastercraft Baja RS ($485):** A premium seat used by desert racers that translates well to overland and trail use. The best lateral support of the three options. The cost is higher and the fixed back requires commitment to a trail-focused use case.\n\nThe WJ rear seat is removable with basic tools \u2014 the seat cushion lifts out and the seat back folds forward. For a dedicated trail rig or overland build, removing the rear seat entirely converts the WJ into a full cargo vehicle. The flat floor with seat out is approximately 48\" long from tailgate to rear of front seats, and 42\" wide between wheel wells. This is a useful flat sleeping platform with a sleeping pad, and the cargo volume rivals small panel vans for trail gear."},{"id":"wj-coolant-flush-wj","title":"Coolant Flush \u2014 PowerTech and 4.0","vehicle":"wj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"cooling","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":70},"tools_required":["drain pan","garden hose","funnel","screwdriver","channel locks"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["cooling","coolant","antifreeze","4.0","4.7","powertech"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar HOAT coolant (MS-9769) \u2014 2 gal","part_number":"MS-9769","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":40},{"name":"Zerex G-05 HOAT coolant \u2014 2 gal alternative","vendor":"Valvoline / Zerex","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Distilled water (1 gal)","vendor":"any grocery","price_estimate_usd":2}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepSpecs \u2014 WJ Cooling System Service","url":"https://jeepspecs.com/models/jeep-grand-cherokee/jeep-grand-cherokee-wj/cooling-system-service/"},{"title":"WJ Bible \u2014 Maintenance information and schedules","url":"https://wjbible.com/service/maintenance_wj.html"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"1999\u20132000 WJs hold about 13 quarts of coolant, 2001\u20132004 hold about 15 quarts. Run HOAT-rated coolant (Mopar MS-9769 or Zerex G-05) at 50/50 with distilled water \u2014 not green ethylene glycol, and not Dex-Cool.","description":"The WJ uses HOAT coolant (Hybrid Organic Acid Technology) from the factory \u2014 it's the amber/yellow stuff, not the green or orange. Mixing chemistries is what kills WJ cooling systems: pour Prestone universal into a HOAT system and the inhibitor package precipitates out, the radiator clogs, and you end up replacing a heater core 20,000 miles later. Stick to MS-9769 spec (Mopar HOAT, Zerex G-05, or Peak Final Charge Global) and use distilled water for the mix.\n\nDrain the radiator at the petcock \u2014 bottom driver-side corner, white plastic, turn by hand (channel locks if it's stuck, but be gentle, the petcock plastic is brittle on these). Open the radiator cap (cold engine only) so it drains faster. The petcock alone gets ~6 quarts out. To get a real flush you also have to drain the block \u2014 there are two block drain plugs on the 4.7L (one per side of the block, hex head, 5/8\") and one on the 4.0L (passenger side block, behind the exhaust manifold). Pulling block drains gets the rest of the old coolant out instead of diluting new coolant with old.\n\nClose everything back up, fill with the recommended capacity (13 qt for 1999\u20132000, 15 qt for 2001\u20132004) of 50/50 HOAT and distilled water. Burp the air out: idle with the heater on full hot, watch the temp, top off the radiator as the air works out. The 4.7L has a known habit of holding an air pocket near the thermostat \u2014 drive the truck through a heat cycle and recheck the overflow bottle the next day.\n\nIf you're past the 100,000-mile mark on factory coolant or never know when the last flush was, this is overdue work. Old HOAT coolant turns acidic and eats the radiator from the inside.","steps":["Cold engine. Open hood, locate the petcock at the radiator bottom driver-side.","Drain pan under the petcock, open radiator cap, open petcock \u2014 drain.","Pull block drain plug(s) \u2014 one on 4.0L (passenger block), two on 4.7L (one each side). Drain.","Close all drains. Reinstall block plugs with fresh sealant on the threads if leaking.","Fill radiator slowly with pre-mixed 50/50 HOAT + distilled water. Heater on high.","Top off as air burps. Cap on, start engine.","Drive a heat cycle, let cool, recheck overflow bottle level, top off."],"pros":["$30\u201370 every 5 years catches a future heater-core replacement","Confirms petcock and drain plugs are still serviceable","Catches early signs of head-gasket trouble on the 4.7 (oil in coolant)"],"cons":["HOAT is more expensive than green ethylene glycol","Block drain plugs can seize on rust-belt trucks","Air pockets on the 4.7 can take two heat cycles to fully burp"]},{"id":"wj-differential-fluid-wj","title":"Differential Fluid \u2014 Dana 30 / 8.25 / 44a","vehicle":"wj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"drivetrain_fluids","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":80},"tools_required":["3/8 ratchet","13mm socket","fluid pump or syringe","drain pan","torque wrench","RTV silicone or new gasket"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["drivetrain","differential","dana30","8.25","dana44a","vari-lok","quadra-drive"],"parts":[{"name":"Valvoline 80W-90 GL-5 gear oil (1 qt) \u2014 front and most rears","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"Mopar 80W-140 synthetic Vari-Lok fluid \u2014 Quadra-Drive II rear","part_number":"5135681AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Mopar Limited Slip friction modifier \u2014 if rear LSD whines on turns","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Differential cover gasket or RTV","vendor":"Fel-Pro / Permatex Ultra Black","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Bible \u2014 Maintenance information and schedules","url":"https://wjbible.com/service/maintenance_wj.html"},{"title":"Morris 4x4 \u2014 Jeep WJ Capacities and Type","url":"https://www.morris4x4center.com/knowledge-center/jeep-wj-capacities-and-type/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Front Dana 30 takes about 1.25 quarts of 80W-90, rear Dana 35 or Chrysler 8.25 takes about 2.5 quarts of 80W-90, and the Dana 44a with Vari-Lok takes the special Mopar 80W-140 synthetic \u2014 2.5 quarts. Use the right fluid in the Vari-Lok rear or it won't engage.","description":"Three rear axle options shipped on the WJ: Dana 35c (base), Chrysler 8.25 (V8 non-Quadra-Drive), and Dana 44a with Vari-Lok (Quadra-Drive and Quadra-Drive II). The front is always a Dana 30 with vacuum-disconnect. Knowing which one you have determines what fluid to buy.\n\nHow to identify your rear: look at the diff cover shape. Dana 35c has a 10-bolt round cover, Dana 44a has a 10-bolt cover that looks similar but the housing is larger (1.32\" axle shafts vs 1.18\"), and the Chrysler 8.25 has a different bolt count \u2014 10 bolts but with a flat oval shape. Or: pop the cover plate inside the spare tire well and look for a 198-, 248-, or 247-style transfer case identifier (NV247 = Quadra-Drive II = Dana 44a Vari-Lok rear). When in doubt, look up the build sheet by VIN.\n\n**Critical:** The Vari-Lok rear uses a viscous coupling that needs Mopar 80W-140 synthetic (part 5135681AA). Pouring regular GL-5 80W-90 in there will not destroy the diff immediately but will reduce coupling effectiveness over time. Don't substitute.\n\nDrain procedure is the same for all three: pull the cover (remove all bolts except the top one, leave it as a hinge to drop the lower edge first), let the fluid drain into a wide pan, clean the magnet on the cover, scrape the old gasket or RTV off the housing and cover surfaces, and check the magnet for excessive metal \u2014 fuzzy is normal, chunks are bad. New gasket or a 1/8\" bead of Permatex Ultra Black RTV around the cover (with circles around each bolt hole), let RTV cure 1 hour before refilling.\n\nThe fill plug is on the side of the housing \u2014 3/8\" square drive on most years. Fluid level is at the bottom of the fill hole. Use a hand pump or fluid syringe \u2014 gravity pour from above is awkward.","steps":["Park level, jack and support each axle individually (you'll fill at the level position).","Drain pan under cover. Remove all cover bolts except the top \u2014 leave the top as a hinge.","Pry the cover bottom free, let fluid drain.","Remove top bolt, take cover off. Clean magnet. Inspect debris.","Scrape old gasket / RTV off both surfaces.","New gasket or RTV bead, reinstall cover. Torque bolts in a star pattern to 30 ft-lbs.","Wait 1 hour for RTV cure (skip if using a gasket).","Remove fill plug. Pump in fluid until it dribbles out \u2014 that's full.","Reinstall fill plug, torque to 25 ft-lbs.","Repeat for the other axle."],"pros":["$30\u201380 for both axles in fluid","Catches a failing Vari-Lok before it grenades","Magnet inspection catches early ring/pinion failure"],"cons":["Wrong fluid in the Vari-Lok rear reduces its lock-up","RTV cure time pushes the job to 2 hours real time","4x4 Quadra-Drive II rears are expensive \u2014 the right fluid is cheap insurance"]},{"id":"wj-oil-change-wj","title":"Oil Change \u2014 4.0L and 4.7L PowerTech","vehicle":"wj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"engine_fluids","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":65},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","13mm socket","oil filter wrench","drain pan","funnel"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["oil","maintenance","4.0","4.7","powertech"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar oil filter (4.0L)","part_number":"MO-090","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Mopar 4.7L H.O. oil filter","part_number":"5281090AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"5W-30 conventional motor oil (6 qt)","vendor":"Valvoline / Mobil / Pennzoil","price_estimate_usd":25}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Bible \u2014 Maintenance information and schedules","url":"https://wjbible.com/service/maintenance_wj.html"},{"title":"Mopar 5281090AB \u2014 4.7L H.O. Oil Filter","url":"https://www.jeepworld.com/products/4-7l-h-o-oil-filter-5281090ab"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 4.0L takes 6 quarts and the 4.7L takes 6 quarts \u2014 both run 5W-30 for daily use, and both are quick driveway jobs once the truck is level and on stands.","description":"The WJ's two engines share an oil capacity (6 quarts including filter) and a recommended weight (5W-30 for most climates, 10W-30 if you live somewhere hot and the engine is high-mileage). What's different is the filter location and the sensitivity to the wrong filter on the 4.7L. The 4.7L PowerTech is known for sludge problems, and a marginal filter contributes \u2014 stick to Mopar 5281090AB or a known-good equivalent (WIX 51068, Fram Ultra XG10575) on the 4.7. The 4.0L is more forgiving but you still want a real filter, not a generic.\n\nPark on level ground and warm the engine for 5 minutes so the oil flows. Lift the front with a floor jack at the front crossmember and set it on jack stands rated for the truck's weight (the WJ is heavier than it looks at around 4,200 lbs). Position the drain pan under the oil pan. The drain plug uses a 13mm socket on both engines. Crack it loose, then back it out by hand the last few turns so it doesn't drop into the pan.\n\nFilter location: 4.0L is on the driver-side rear of the engine block, accessible from below. 4.7L is on the driver-side front, also from below but trickier \u2014 there's not much clearance. An oil filter wrench helps both. Hand-tighten the new filter (oil the gasket first), then snug it a quarter-turn past contact. Don't gorilla it on.\n\nRefill from the top with 6 quarts, run for 30 seconds, check the dipstick, top off as needed. Reset the oil indicator if your model has one (most pre-2003 WJs don't \u2014 they want you to remember).","steps":["Warm engine 5 minutes, then shut down.","Lift front with floor jack on the front crossmember, set on jack stands.","Position drain pan, remove 13mm drain plug with a clean socket.","Let oil drain fully (~5 minutes).","Remove the oil filter \u2014 driver-side rear (4.0L) or driver-side front (4.7L).","Oil the gasket on the new filter, hand-thread it on, snug a quarter-turn past contact.","Reinstall drain plug \u2014 torque to 25 ft-lbs.","Lower the truck, refill with 6 quarts of 5W-30.","Run engine 30 seconds, check for leaks at the filter and plug, check dipstick.","Top off if needed."],"pros":["$30\u201340 in parts on the 4.0L, $40\u201365 on the 4.7L","Under an hour with stands and a drain pan","Catches the 4.7L sludge problem early if you cut the filter open at each change"],"cons":["4.7L filter location is cramped","Aftermarket filters with weak bypass valves accelerate 4.7L sludge","WJ does not have a factory drain interval indicator \u2014 track it yourself"]},{"id":"wj-spark-plugs-wj","title":"Spark Plugs \u2014 4.0 and 4.7 PowerTech","vehicle":"wj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"ignition","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":90},"tools_required":["5/8 spark plug socket","3/8 ratchet","torque wrench","swivel extensions","anti-seize","dielectric grease","gap tool"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["ignition","tune-up","4.0","4.7","powertech"],"parts":[{"name":"Champion RC12MCC4 \u2014 4.0L (set of 6)","part_number":"Champion 439","vendor":"Champion / Mopar SPRC12MCC4","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Champion RC7PYCB4 \u2014 4.7L Standard (set of 8)","part_number":"Champion 3340","vendor":"Champion / Mopar SPRC7PYCB4","price_estimate_usd":70},{"name":"Champion RC7PYCB4 \u2014 4.7L HO (set of 8)","part_number":"Champion 3340","vendor":"Champion / Mopar SPRC7PYCB4","price_estimate_usd":85}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepSpecs \u2014 WJ Spark Plugs reference","url":"https://jeepspecs.com/wj-generation/wj-jeep-parts-equipment/spark-plugs/"},{"title":"JeepSpecs \u2014 4.7L HO Engine","url":"https://wjjeeps.com/engine.htm"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Use the exact plug Chrysler specified \u2014 Champion copper RC12MCC4 on the 4.0L (gap 0.035\"), Champion platinum RC7PYCB4 on the 4.7L (gap 0.040\"). Substituting \"upgrades\" can cause pre-ignition on the 4.0 and rough running on the 4.7.","description":"The 4.0L straight-six and the 4.7L PowerTech V8 both use unusual spark plug specs, and both punish builders who try to upgrade to a \"better\" plug. Don't run NGK Iridium IX on either engine \u2014 they will work, but the ground electrode geometry is wrong and you'll get misfires under load. The factory Champions are not premium plugs, but they are the right plugs.\n\nThe 4.0L: 6 plugs in a straight line on the passenger side of the head. Pull the boots straight up (twist first if they've been on a while \u2014 silicone bonds to ceramic). 5/8\" plug socket, 3/8\" ratchet, swivel extension makes it more manageable. Plugs gap to 0.035\". Anti-seize on the threads (small amount \u2014 too much can ground the plug), dielectric grease in the boot. Torque to 27 ft-lbs.\n\nThe 4.7L: 8 plugs, one per cylinder, plus a coil-on-plug ignition system. Pull the harness connector at each coil, remove the 7mm coil bolt, lift the coil, then unscrew the plug. The Standard 4.7L (VIN code N) uses RC12MCC4 \u2014 copper core, gap 0.035\". The HO 4.7L (VIN code J, 2002\u20132004) uses RC7PYCB4 \u2014 platinum-tipped, gap 0.040\". Check the VIN before you buy: the eighth character tells you which engine (N or J). Torque to 13 ft-lbs on the 4.7 \u2014 these aluminum heads will strip if you overtighten, so use a torque wrench, not a feel-it tighten.\n\nService interval: 30,000 miles on the 4.0, 30,000 miles on the 4.7 Standard, 100,000 miles on the 4.7 HO platinums (though most owners do them at 60\u201380k since the plugs are cheap insurance against a misfire on a coil-on-plug system).","steps":["Engine cold \u2014 never pull plugs on a hot aluminum head (4.7L).","Disconnect battery negative.","**4.0L:** Pull each boot, 5/8\" socket out the plug, gap new plug to 0.035\", small dab of anti-seize, torque to 27 ft-lbs. Dielectric grease the new boot interior, reinstall.","**4.7L:** Unplug coil connector, remove 7mm coil bolt, lift coil, plug socket on the plug, swap, gap to 0.040\", torque to 13 ft-lbs, reinstall coil and connector.","Reconnect battery, start engine, listen for misfires."],"pros":["$25\u201390 in parts depending on engine","Catches a soon-to-fail coil on the 4.7 (look for white corrosion on the boot tip)","Restores idle quality on a 100k-mile WJ"],"cons":["Wrong plug = misfires or pre-ignition","4.7L coil-on-plug system means coils can fail and look like a plug problem","Aluminum heads on the 4.7 strip easily if you over-torque"]},{"id":"recovery-points-wj","title":"Recovery Points on the WJ Grand Cherokee Unibody","vehicle":"wj","category":"recovery","subcategory":"recovery points","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":250},"tools_required":["torque wrench","sockets","wire brush"],"skills_required":["recovery rigging basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["recovery","recovery points","tow hooks","D-rings","shackles","unibody"],"parts":[{"name":"3/4\" rated D-ring shackle (pair)","vendor":"Warn or ARB","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Receiver-mount D-ring adapter (2\")","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Front bumper with D-ring tabs (WJ)","vendor":"Armor Works","price_estimate_usd":780}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Recovery Point Guide \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"},{"title":"Recovery Hardware Safety Ratings \u2014 ARB","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"}],"alternatives":["winch-wj","front-bumper-wj","rear-bumper-wj"],"summary":"The WJ unibody has engineered recovery points \u2014 the front frame horns and the rear subframe receiver hitch area. Using anything else for a recovery pull risks tearing out bodywork or creating a projectile. Know what you have and what it is rated for before you need it on the trail.","description":"The WJ front frame horns are the forward structural elements of the unibody. The factory tow hooks (black painted hooks on either side of the bumper, visible on most trims) are bolted to these horns and are rated for the towing loads Jeep tested. They are adequate for a straight-back snatch strap recovery pull.\n\nInspect the frame horn condition on any WJ before relying on the tow hooks for a recovery. The horns are a rust-prone area on northern-state vehicles (see rust-wj) \u2014 a hook bolted to a corroded horn is not trustworthy. Wire brush the visible metal and probe with a screwdriver; if the horn is solid, the tow hook is a valid recovery point.\n\nThe better front recovery solution is an aftermarket steel front bumper with integrated D-ring tab shackle mounts. D-ring tabs welded into a steel bumper that mounts correctly to the frame horns provide a rated, accessible attachment point that is far easier to use quickly than the factory hook. An Armor Works or similar WJ bumper includes these tabs as standard.\n\nThe Class III 2\" receiver hitch, mounted to the rear subframe crossmember, is the correct rear recovery attachment. Use a receiver-mount D-ring adapter ($35) in the 2\" receiver with a hitch pin to retain it. Connect a rated 3/4\" shackle to the D-ring. This setup provides a clean, rated attachment point that handles snatch strap loads correctly.\n\nRate the receiver for recovery at approximately 50% of the advertised tongue weight \u2014 the dynamic load from a snatch strap is significantly higher than the static rating. For the WJ Class III hitch, that gives a 3,500\u20134,000 lb safe recovery pull \u2014 adequate for a straight pull but on the conservative side for a steep, stuck-in-the-ruts scenario. For those situations, a front winch is the preferred tool.\n\n**The rear plastic bumper cover** \u2014 not a recovery point. It is plastic.\n**The spare tire carrier or under-floor spare tire well** \u2014 not a recovery point.\n**Trailer hitch ball** \u2014 remove the ball mount and use a D-ring adapter instead.\n**Body panels or door hinges** \u2014 never.\n**Suspension components** \u2014 never attach to control arms, sway bar links, or differential.\n\nUse rated hardware only. WLL (working load limit) ratings on shackles and straps must exceed the vehicle weight. For a 4,200 lb WJ, use 3/4\" shackles rated at 4.75 tons WLL minimum."},{"id":"winch-wj","title":"Winch Selection for the WJ Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wj","category":"recovery","subcategory":"winch","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":900},"tools_required":["sockets","torque wrench","wire cutters","crimper"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","electrical wiring"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["winch","recovery","synthetic rope","Warn","Smittybilt","self-recovery"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn Zeon 10-S 10,000 lb winch (synthetic)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":820},{"name":"Warn VR12000-S 12,000 lb winch (synthetic)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":570},{"name":"Smittybilt X2-10000 10,000 lb winch (synthetic)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Superwinch Talon 9.5i 9,500 lb winch","vendor":"Superwinch","price_estimate_usd":330}],"citations":[{"title":"Winch Selection Guide \u2014 WarnIndustries.com","url":"https://www.warnindustries.com"},{"title":"WJ Winch and Bumper Guide \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"}],"alternatives":["recovery-points-wj","front-bumper-wj","winch-wiring-wj"],"summary":"A 4,200 lb WJ needs at least a 10,000 lb winch. Synthetic rope is lighter, safer when it breaks, and easier to handle than steel cable. Get the bumper first \u2014 a winch ordered before the bumper sits in a box in the garage.","description":"The standard rule for winch sizing is 1.5x the vehicle's gross weight. A loaded WJ with gear and a full tank runs 4,200\u20134,500 lbs \u2014 figure 4,500 lbs as the working number. 1.5x 4,500 = 6,750 lbs minimum. A 10,000 lb winch gives a comfortable safety margin and handles the worst-case scenario: pulling the WJ straight up a steep, muddy slope from full stick.\n\nFor a WJ that runs technical rock or is frequently used in deep mud or water crossings, a 12,000 lb winch is worth the modest cost premium. The difference between a 10K and 12K Warn is roughly $100 and the extra capacity gives you more options on the first layer of rope wrap (where rated capacity is lowest).\n\n**Synthetic rope** (Dyneema or UHMWPE construction) has replaced steel cable as the default on trail-use winches. The reasons are practical:\n\n- When synthetic rope breaks under load, it drops to the ground. When steel cable breaks under load, it becomes a high-energy projectile. This is the most important safety difference.\n- Synthetic rope weighs significantly less than an equivalent steel cable \u2014 important for a heavy unibody SUV.\n- Synthetic rope is easier to handle \u2014 no wire fragments, no rust, no gloves required.\n- Synthetic rope does not develop kinks the way steel cable does.\n\nThe trade-off: synthetic rope is more susceptible to abrasion from sharp rocks and degrades in UV over years. Keep it covered on the drum when not in use and inspect regularly for fraying or core damage. Replace when visible wear is found \u2014 synthetic rope repair is not trail-field-compatible.\n\n**Warn VR12000-S ($570):** The mid-tier Warn with a 12,000 lb rating and factory synthetic rope. The VR series uses a Series wound motor and a proven gear train. Warn's customer service and parts availability are unmatched. For most WJ trail builds, this is the right winch.\n\n**Warn Zeon 10-S ($820):** The premium Warn with a more compact build and a lower profile drum. Worth it if bumper clearance is tight.\n\n**Smittybilt X2-10000 ($350):** A functional budget winch that has proven reliable for moderate use. The motor and gears are adequate for typical recoveries. Not rated for sustained commercial or competition use, but for the occasional trail recovery it performs acceptably.\n\nOrder the bumper before the winch and confirm the winch plate dimensions. Winch plate width (the opening on the bumper) must match the winch drum width. Most aftermarket WJ bumpers with winch plates specify compatible winch brands and drum widths \u2014 match these before purchasing."},{"id":"wj-death-wobble","title":"Death Wobble Diagnosis","vehicle":"wj","category":"steering","subcategory":"death_wobble","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","pry bar","sockets and wrenches"],"skills_required":["methodical diagnosis","torque-spec discipline"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2001","2002-2004"],"tags":["steering","safety-critical","death-wobble"],"parts":[{"name":"Track bar (heavy-duty)","vendor":"Iron Rock/Synergy","price_estimate_usd":160},{"name":"Tie-rod ends / drag link","vendor":"Moog","price_estimate_usd":150},{"name":"Front ball joints","vendor":"Moog/Spicer","price_estimate_usd":130}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ drivetrain reference \u2014 Iron Rock Off Road","url":"https://www.ironrockoffroad.com/category/wj-99-04-grand-cherokee-7726.html"},{"title":"JeepSpecs WJ specifications","url":"https://jeepspecs.com/wj-generation/specifications/maintenance-information-and-schedules/"}],"alternatives":["wj-lift-kit-guide"],"summary":"Like the ZJ before it, the WJ uses a coil-sprung solid front axle located by a track bar, and it is every bit as prone to death wobble when a joint wears. The shake is the symptom; the worn track bar, tie-rod end, or ball joint is the cause. Find and fix the worn part \u2014 do not drive it and do not mask it with a damper.","description":"Death wobble on the WJ is a stop-driving-it-now problem. The feedback loop is identical to the ZJ: a worn locating joint lets the front axle oscillate, the oscillation feeds through the steering, and it will not settle until you brake to a crawl. The WJ's most common trigger is the track bar \u2014 both ends wear and the frame-side mount elongates. Check it first by trying to move the bar by hand at each end and at the bracket.\n\nThen go through the front end in order, wheels off the ground: tie-rod ends and drag link for play, ball joints at 12-and-6, control-arm bushings for cracks. The WJ also runs an upper and lower control arm per side; collapsed bushings there let the axle shift and mimic a steering fault. Re-torque the track bar and control-arm hardware to factory spec \u2014 a loose bolt behaves exactly like a worn joint. Out-of-balance tires and loose wheel bearings round out the checklist.\n\nSkip the \"bigger steering stabilizer fixes death wobble\" advice. It hides the onset and lets the real culprit keep wearing until something lets go at speed. Replace the worn joint, torque to spec, and the WJ tracks straight.","steps":["Take the WJ off the highway until the diagnosis is done.","Check the track bar ends and frame bracket for play first.","Inspect tie-rod ends, drag link, ball joints, and upper/lower control-arm bushings.","Re-torque track bar and control-arm bolts; check wheel bearings and tire balance.","Replace worn parts and road-test at increasing speed in a safe area."]},{"id":"wj-lift-kit-guide","title":"Lift Kit Guide","vehicle":"wj","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":5,"high":14},"cost_usd":{"low":300,"high":2200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","spring compressor","breaker bar","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["wrenching","suspension geometry basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2001","2002-2004"],"tags":["suspension","lift","tires"],"parts":[{"name":"Coil lift kit (3-4 in)","vendor":"Iron Rock/Rusty's/Clayton","price_estimate_usd":750},{"name":"Adjustable front track bar","vendor":"Iron Rock/Synergy","price_estimate_usd":160},{"name":"Extended brake lines + control-arm correction","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":150}],"citations":[{"title":"Iron Rock Off Road WJ suspension","url":"https://www.ironrockoffroad.com/category/wj-99-04-grand-cherokee-7726.html"},{"title":"Quadratec WJ suspension catalog","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/"}],"alternatives":["wj-death-wobble"],"summary":"The WJ is coil-sprung all around with a solid front axle, and like the ZJ it lifts with coils and longer shocks. A 3\u20134 inch kit clears 31\u201332\" tires and is the practical ceiling for a street-and-trail WJ. The limiting factor is geometry and the independent-feeling Quadra-Drive driveline, not spring options.","description":"Verdict: a 3\u20134\" coil kit with an adjustable front track bar and control-arm correction is the honest WJ build, on 31\u201332s. The WJ shares the ZJ's vulnerability \u2014 raise the track-bar-located front axle without re-centering it and you set the stage for death wobble. The adjustable track bar re-centers the axle, and adjustable or correction-bracketed control arms put the caster back where the steering wants it. Both matter above 3 inches.\n\nPlan for the supporting parts: extended brake lines for the added droop, longer shocks matched to the lift, and on Quadra-Drive trucks, attention to the front driveshaft and the Vari-Lok/Quadra-Trac front diff angle. The 4.7L V8 trucks are nose-heavy, so do not under-spring the front; use the V8-rate coils if your kit offers them.\n\nKeep tire goals realistic. 31s wake the WJ up with little fuss. 32s need the full 4\" and trimming. 33s push the unibody, the brakes, and the Dana 30 front past their comfort zone \u2014 possible, but a build with regearing and trimming, not a weekend bolt-on. Match your gears to the tire and remember the front Dana 30 must match the rear ratio.","steps":["Lift, support the unibody, and unload the coils one corner at a time.","Install coils and matched shocks (V8-rate front coils on 4.7L trucks).","Fit an adjustable front track bar; correct caster with adjustable/bracketed control arms.","Add extended brake lines; check front driveshaft and diff angle on Quadra-Drive trucks.","Alignment afterward to set caster and toe."]},{"id":"diagnostics-wj","title":"OBD2 Diagnosis on the WJ Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wj","category":"tools_diagnostic","subcategory":"OBD diagnostics","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":120},"tools_required":["OBD2 scan tool"],"skills_required":["basic diagnosis","code reading"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["OBD2","diagnostics","check engine","fault codes","scan tool","live data","4.0L","4.7L"],"parts":[{"name":"Autel AL519 OBD2 scanner","vendor":"Autel","price_estimate_usd":70},{"name":"Autel MX808 scan tool (live data)","vendor":"Autel","price_estimate_usd":185},{"name":"BlueDriver Bluetooth OBD2 adapter","vendor":"BlueDriver","price_estimate_usd":100}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Grand Cherokee OBD2 Code Reference \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"},{"title":"Chrysler OBD2 Code Guide \u2014 AllPar.com","url":"https://www.allpar.com"}],"alternatives":["40l-engine-zj","wj-47-cooling-common-problems"],"summary":"All WJ Grand Cherokees (1999\u20132004) use standard OBD2 \u2014 any $30 scan tool reads codes, any $100 Bluetooth adapter gives live data on your phone. The challenge is not reading the code; it is knowing which codes point to real problems versus sensor failures.","description":"The WJ OBD2 port is the standard 16-pin trapezoid connector under the driver's side dashboard, left of center. All WJ model years (1999\u20132004) use standard OBD2 that works with any generic scan tool. Chrysler's implementation is fully compliant \u2014 no proprietary adapter or dealer tool is required for basic code reading and clearing.\n\nFor simple code reads and clears, a $30\u201370 handheld scanner (Autel AL519, Actron CP9600) is sufficient. For live data streams \u2014 reading coolant temperature, O2 sensor voltage, fuel trims, and misfire counts in real time \u2014 step up to a Bluetooth adapter paired with an app (BlueDriver, $100) or a mid-tier scan tool (Autel MX808). Live data capability is important for separating intermittent faults from permanent ones.\n\n**P0344 \u2014 Crankshaft Position Sensor (CPS):** The most common 4.0L WJ fault code. Symptoms: no-start when hot, intermittent stall, random stall at idle. Replace the CPS with a Mopar or Standard Motor Products unit. Do not use no-name sensors.\n\n**P0740 \u2014 TCC Solenoid:** Torque converter clutch solenoid circuit fault on the 45RFE transmission. Causes: harsh engagement or slipping between 3rd and 4th gear, shudder at highway speed. Check fluid condition first \u2014 contaminated ATF causes TCC solenoid issues.\n\n**P0128 \u2014 Coolant Temperature Below Threshold:** Thermostat stuck open. The 4.0L thermostat is a cheap part ($18); replace it when this code appears.\n\n**P1389 / P1388 \u2014 ASD Relay / Gas Pedal Position Sensor:** The Auto Shutdown relay controls fuel pump, injectors, and ignition coil. A failing ASD relay causes intermittent stall and no-start. Swap the relay (it is a standard SPDT relay) before condemning the PCM.\n\n**P0300 \u2014 Random Misfire:** Can be caused by spark plugs, plug wires, coil packs, fuel pressure, or a vacuum leak. Start with plugs and wires; check fuel pressure; test for vacuum leaks.\n\n**P0300 \u2014 Multiple Cylinder Misfire:** On the 4.7L, deteriorated ignition coil boots are the primary cause. The coil-on-plug system uses a rubber boot to contact the spark plug; old boots crack and allow the spark to arc to the cylinder head rather than the plug. Inspect and replace the coil boots ($8\u201312 each) before replacing the coils themselves.\n\n**P0122 / P0123 \u2014 Throttle Position Sensor:** The 4.7L's TPS fails at moderate mileage. Symptoms: erratic idle, sudden power loss, rough shift quality. Replacement is straightforward.\n\n**P0153 \u2014 O2 Sensor Slow Response (Bank 2):** The 4.7L V8 has O2 sensors on both banks. Slow response codes indicate an aging sensor that should be replaced. Confirm with live data \u2014 a healthy O2 sensor switches between 0.1V and 0.9V multiple times per second.\n\nReading fault codes tells you what the PCM detected. Live data tells you what is happening right now. Two examples where live data matters:\n\n**Coolant temperature:** A P0128 (coolant temp below threshold) with live data showing 185\u00b0F at operating temperature means the thermostat opened early but the system got warm enough. A reading stuck at 140\u00b0F at operating temperature means the thermostat is not closing and the engine is running cold \u2014 this reduces fuel efficiency and accelerates wear.\n\n**Fuel trims:** Short-term and long-term fuel trim numbers in live data show whether the PCM is adding or subtracting fuel to compensate for a rich or lean condition. Long-term trim over +10% means the engine is running lean (vacuum leak, weak fuel pump, clogged injector). Over -10% means rich (failing O2 sensor, leaking injector). This guides diagnosis more efficiently than replacing parts based on codes alone."},{"id":"tire-sizing-wj","title":"Tire Fitment Guide for the WJ Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wj","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tire sizing","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":600,"high":1400},"tools_required":["torque wrench","floor jack","tape measure"],"skills_required":["basic wheel knowledge"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["tires","tire sizing","fitment","lift","fender trimming","33s","35s"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich KO2 33x12.5R16 (set of 4)","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":260},{"name":"Falken Wildpeak AT3W 33x12.5R17 (set of 4)","vendor":"Falken","price_estimate_usd":215},{"name":"Mickey Thompson Baja Boss 35x12.5R17 (set of 4)","vendor":"Mickey Thompson","price_estimate_usd":285}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Tire Fitment Guide \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"}],"alternatives":["wheels-wj","regear-wj","wj-lift-kit-guide"],"summary":"The WJ's solid axle and full-size wheelbase (105.9\\\") make it a good platform for larger tires \u2014 but fitment limits are real and cutting fenders to run 35s on a daily driver adds clearance concerns. Know the trade-offs before ordering.","description":"The factory WJ ran 225/75R16 (approximately 29.3\" diameter) on base models and 245/70R16 (approximately 29.7\") on higher trims. These are conservative sizes that leave substantial clearance in the wheel wells.\n\nEven on stock suspension, the WJ has enough clearance for a modest tire upgrade. A 235/75R16 (approximately 30.3\") or 245/75R16 (approximately 30.5\") fits without rubbing under most conditions. Going to a 31x10.5 on the stock suspension works but will show contact at full steering lock on the front mud flap \u2014 trim the flap edge and it clears.\n\nA 2\u20133\" lift kit (quality spring/strut upgrade \u2014 see wj-lift-kit-guide) creates enough clearance for 33x12.5 tires on 8\"-wide wheels with 3.75\" backspacing. This is the most common WJ tire upgrade and the practical sweet spot. The 33x12.5 provides a significant increase in traction footprint and ground clearance without requiring aggressive fender modification.\n\nTrim the inner plastic mud flaps at the rear of the front wheel wells for clearance at full steering lock. This is a 10-minute job with a utility knife and costs nothing. No metal cutting is required at 33\" on a properly-lifted WJ.\n\n**Regear at 33\":** Factory 3.55 gears with 33s are noticeably sluggish. Adding 4.10 gears restores proper power delivery and highway RPM. See regear-wj.\n\nA 4\" lift (quality lift, not a spacer \u2014 the SYE is required here, see sye-wj) creates enough clearance for a 35x12.5 tire with modification. The inner front fender area requires cutting to prevent contact at full droop and full steering lock \u2014 typically 1\u20132\" of inner fender plastic needs to come out. This is achievable but makes the wheel well less protected against debris.\n\nAt 35\", the NV247 transfer case output chain strength becomes a consideration under sustained load. The transfer case chain (see transfer-case-wj) handles 35s on the trail, but aggressive wheeling at 35s with a stock-chain NV247 will accelerate wear. Plan for chain service as part of the 35-tire build.\n\n**Regear at 35\":** 4.56:1 gears are the correct ratio for 35\" tires. Do not run 35s long-term on 3.55 gears.\n\nGoing beyond 35\" on the WJ requires major fender modification, different wheel offset to clear the fenders, alignment concerns, and stress on the transfer case and front Dana 30 (which approaches its limits at 35\" tires being flogged). A 37-tire WJ exists but it is a dedicated trail rig with significant modifications and compromised street manners. For most builders, 33s or 35s is the right endpoint."},{"id":"wheels-wj","title":"Wheel Selection for the WJ Grand Cherokee","vehicle":"wj","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"wheels","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":240,"high":900},"tools_required":["torque wrench","lug wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wheel knowledge"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1999-2004"],"tags":["wheels","bolt pattern","backspacing","5x4.5","Method","Black Rhino","steel"],"parts":[{"name":"Method 305 NV 17x8.5 (5x4.5)","vendor":"Method Race Wheels","price_estimate_usd":210},{"name":"Black Rhino Arsenal 17x9 (5x4.5)","vendor":"Black Rhino","price_estimate_usd":195},{"name":"Pro Comp 69 Series 15x8 steel (5x4.5)","vendor":"Pro Comp","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Mopar Trail 15x8 steel (5x4.5)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":65}],"citations":[{"title":"WJ Wheel Fitment Guide \u2014 Grand Cherokee Forum","url":"https://www.grandcherokeeassociation.com"}],"alternatives":["tire-sizing-wj"],"summary":"The WJ uses the 5x4.5\" (5x114.3mm) bolt pattern \u2014 same as most Wranglers \u2014 so the aftermarket for this bolt pattern is enormous. The factory wheels are 6\" wide, which is too narrow for 33x12.5 tires. Go to at least 8\" wide.","description":"The WJ Grand Cherokee uses **5x4.5\" (5x114.3mm)** bolt pattern with a 71.5mm center bore. This is the same pattern as the YJ, TJ, JK, and JL Wrangler. Any wheel designed for the TJ Wrangler with the correct backspacing and hub bore will work on the WJ.\n\nLug nut thread is M14x1.5. Match lug nut seat to wheel seat type \u2014 most aftermarket wheels use a 60-degree conical seat.\n\nThe factory WJ 16\" aluminum wheels are 6\u20137\" wide depending on trim. A 33x12.5\" tire on a 6\" wide wheel creates a balloon profile \u2014 the tire is wider than the wheel, the sidewalls bulge outward, and the handling and bead retention are compromised. Run at least 8\" wide for 33x12.5 tires.\n\nA 17x9\" wheel is the standard modern trail setup for the WJ. The extra half-inch over 8.5\" improves tire profile shape and bead retention under load. It also opens up more tire choices in the 285/70R17 and 33x12.5R17 sizes.\n\n**3.75\" backspacing on a 17x9\"** is the well-established WJ clearance spec for 33s with a 2\u20133\" lift. This positions the tire correctly relative to the wheel well without fouling on the spring perch, control arms, or inner fender at full droop and full steering lock.\n\nGoing to 4.5\" backspacing on a 17x9\" tucks the tire too far inboard and risks contact with the coil spring at full droop on a lifted WJ. Going under 3.5\" backspacing on a wide wheel pushes the tire too far outside the fender edge.\n\n**Steel wheels** are the trail-use choice \u2014 durable, inexpensive to replace, and fixable in the field with heat and a hammer. Pro Comp and Mopar Trail both make 15x8\" steel wheels in 5x4.5\" for around $65\u201375 each.\n\n**Alloy wheels** from Method and Black Rhino are the aesthetic and weight upgrade. The Method 305 NV is a purpose-built trail wheel with a thick lip, reinforced spoke base, and a machined steel insert at the bead area \u2014 it handles rock impacts significantly better than a decorative alloy wheel. At $210 each, it is worth the premium for a WJ that sees serious trail use."}];